 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Subject and object",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "In \"the girl calls the slave\", the girl does the calling: she is the <b>subject</b>. The slave has it done to him: he is the <b>object</b>. Latin shows which is which by the ending of each noun."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">puella</u> <u class=\"v\">servum</u> vocat.",
      "en": "<b>The girl</b> calls <b>the slave</b>.",
      "note": "<em>puella</em> is nominative, so she is the subject. <em>servum</em> is accusative, so he is the object."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"v\">puellam</u> <u class=\"c\">servus</u> vocat.",
      "en": "<b>The slave</b> calls <b>the girl</b>.",
      "note": "The endings have swapped, so the jobs have swapped. The order of the words did not decide it; the endings did."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "The <b>nominative</b> case is for the subject: the person or thing doing the verb.",
      "The <b>accusative</b> case is for the object: the person or thing the verb is done to.",
      "Latin has no word for \"the\" or \"a\". Add whichever sounds right in English: <em>servus</em> is \"the slave\" or \"a slave\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
