  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating a sentence",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the verb, usually last. Its ending tells you whether the subject is one or more than one.",
      "Find the nominative: that is the subject. Translate it first.",
      "Then the verb, then the accusative: subject, verb, object, the order English needs.",
      "If there is no nominative, the subject is inside the verb: <em>servos vocat</em>, \"he calls the slaves\"."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">domini</u> <u class=\"v\">equos</u> amant.",
      "en": "<b>The masters</b> love <b>the horses</b>.",
      "note": "<em>amant</em> is \"they\", so the subject is plural: <em>domini</em>, nominative plural."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"v\">cibum</u> parat.",
      "en": "<b>She is preparing the food.</b>",
      "note": "No nominative, so the subject comes from <em>-t</em>: he or she."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Do not translate in the Latin order. <em>servum puella vocat</em> is \"the girl calls the slave\", whichever word comes first.",
      "<em>-i</em> on a 2nd declension noun is plural: <em>servi</em> is \"slaves\", not \"slave\".",
      "<em>-ae</em> and <em>-as</em> are both plural in the 1st declension: <em>-ae</em> for the subject, <em>-as</em> for the object.",
      "A plural verb, ending <em>-nt</em>, goes with a plural subject."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
